  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Find the Laplace transform of ${f}^{\u2032}(t)$ for $s>3$ given that the function $f(t)$ is continuous over $[0,\\infty ),$ $f(0)=5,$ and\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=\\frac{5s-6}{{s}^{2}-3s},\\,s>3.\n$$\n\nAssume that ${f}^{\u2032}$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty ).$",
  "worked_solution": "Suppose $F(s)=L{f(t)}$ where $f(t)$ is a continuous function of exponential order $a$ on $[0,\\infty ).$ If ${f}^{\u2032}(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty ),$ then\n\n$$\nL{{f}^{\u2032}(t)}=sL{f(t)}-f(0).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the Laplace transform of ${f}^{\u2032}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{f}^{\u2032}(t)} & =sL{f(t)}-f(0) \\\\  & =s\\cdot \\frac{5s-6}{{s}^{2}-3s}-5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5{s}^{2}-6s}{{s}^{2}-3s}-5 \\\\  & =\\frac{9s}{{s}^{2}-3s} \\\\  & =\\frac{9s}{s(s-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{s-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>3.$",
